  "description": "Compute the Skip-Gram Negative Sampling (SGNS) loss for a target-context pair with negative samples.\n\nSGNS replaces the intractable full-vocabulary softmax denominator with K binary logistic regressions.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{SGNS}} = -\\log \\sigma(\\mathbf{v}_c^\\top \\mathbf{v}_t) - \\sum_{k=1}^K \\log \\sigma(-\\mathbf{v}_{n_k}^\\top \\mathbf{v}_t)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute dot product between target and true context vector\n2. Compute dot products between target and negative sample vectors\n3. Apply sigmoid cross-entropy and sum negative log-likelihoods\n\n### Returns\nScalar SGNS loss rounded to 6 decimal places.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Word2Vec\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Word2Vec** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nSGNS replaces the intractable full-vocabulary softmax denominator with K binary logistic regressions. In this problem, the goal is to: compute the skip-gram negative sampling (sgns) loss for a target-context pair with negative samples.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{SGNS}} = -\\log \\sigma(\\mathbf{v}_c^\\top \\mathbf{v}_t) - \\sum_{k=1}^K \\log \\sigma(-\\mathbf{v}_{n_k}^\\top \\mathbf{v}_t)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute dot product between target and true context vector\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute dot products between target and negative sample vectors\n3. **Phase 3**: Apply sigmoid cross-entropy and sum negative log-likelihoods\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
